{"id": "2026-11-04-news-democrats-take-both-houses-of-congress", "date": "2026-11-04", "stream": "news", "headline": "Democrats take both houses of Congress", "summary": "Democrats won majorities in both the US House and Senate in the 3 November 2026 midterms, flipping Senate seats in Texas, Kansas, Maine and Michigan.", "url": "https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/11/4/democrats-win-control-of-house-and-senate-key-us-midterms-takeaways", "source_domain": "aljazeera.com", "tier": "T2", "tags": [], "topics": ["politics", "world"], "importance": 3, "display_body": "Democrats won control of both the House and the Senate in the US midterm elections on Tuesday 3 November, the first time since 2006 that a sitting Republican president has lost both chambers. By the Associated Press tally relayed by Al Jazeera on Wednesday morning, Democrats had won 228 House seats and led in 34 more (218 is a majority), and had won 54 Senate seats (51 is a majority); counting continues and several races are uncalled. They flipped Senate seats in states Republicans have long held: James Talarico beat Ken Paxton in Texas, Adam Hamilton became the first Democrat elected to the Senate from Kansas since 1932, Troy Jackson unseated Susan Collins in Maine, and Abdul El-Sayed beat Mike Rogers in Michigan to become the first Muslim elected to the Senate. Speaking to Fox News, Donald Trump congratulated the Democrats and said he would accept the results, but added \"I wasn't on the ballot\", and he posted an image of a red cap reading \"Veto\" on Truth Social.", "why": "from January, legislation, budgets and investigations will need a Democratic Congress, so Trump will have to rely on his veto and on executive action for the second half of his term, including over the Iran war.", "thread_id": "2026-11-04-news-democrats-take-both-houses-of-congress", "first_seen_date": "2026-11-04", "event_date": "2026-11-03", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["United States", "Congress", "Donald Trump", "Democratic Party"]}
{"id": "2026-11-04-news-trump-says-israel-military-aid-will-end", "date": "2026-11-04", "stream": "news", "headline": "Trump says Israel military aid will end", "summary": "Trump said on Fox News the US will end its roughly $3.8bn annual military aid to Israel by the end of 2026, two years before the memorandum expires.", "url": "https://www.dw.com/en/us-to-cut-nearly-4-billion-in-israel-military-aid-trump-says/a-79848860", "source_domain": "dw.com", "tier": "T2", "tags": ["single-source"], "topics": ["geopolitics"], "importance": 2, "display_body": "On Tuesday 3 November, Donald Trump said in a Fox News interview that the US will stop paying Israel's roughly $3.8 billion a year in military aid by the end of 2026, and that Israel is \"fine\" with this. The aid comes under a 10-year memorandum of understanding that began in 2019 and runs until September 2028. Trump said he and Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu had \"made a deal months ago\", but he did not explain how the agreement would end early, and no written US or Israeli statement has been published. He said Israel could still buy US weapons \"whenever they want\". Opposition leader Gadi Eisenkot, who is trying to form a coalition to replace Netanyahu, said Israel's defence establishment had learned of the change \"through the media\".", "why": "this would be the biggest change in US military support for Israel in decades, but for now it rests only on a TV interview.", "thread_id": "2026-11-04-news-trump-says-israel-military-aid-will-end", "first_seen_date": "2026-11-04", "event_date": "2026-11-03", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["United States", "Israel", "Donald Trump", "Benjamin Netanyahu"]}
{"id": "2026-11-04-news-israel-certifies-election-rejects-fraud-claims", "date": "2026-11-04", "stream": "news", "headline": "Israel certifies election, rejects fraud claims", "summary": "Israel's Central Elections Committee certified the 27 October Knesset election results on 4 November after finding no fraud.", "url": "https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/11/4/israel-vote-results-confirmed-as-election-commission-rejects-fraud-claims", "source_domain": "aljazeera.com", "tier": "T2", "tags": ["single-source"], "topics": ["politics", "geopolitics"], "importance": 2, "display_body": "On Wednesday 4 November, Israel's Central Elections Committee certified the results of the Knesset election of 27 October, saying a review of 9,069 polling stations found no irregularities that changed the outcome. Committee chair Noam Sohlberg, a Supreme Court justice, handed the results to President Isaac Herzog and said those casting doubt on them pose \"a real danger\" to Israel's democracy. Likud and Shas abstained in the committee's vote, and Sohlberg cast the deciding vote. Netanyahu and his allies have claimed, without evidence, that Arab-led parties \"stole\" the election; National Security Minister Itamar Ben-Gvir repeated that claim and said his Otzma Yehudit party would vote against approving the results. Herzog is meeting the parties, which have not yet formed a majority.", "why": "the certification removes the formal basis for Likud's challenge and moves the fight to coalition talks, where Netanyahu's opponents are lining up behind Eisenkot.", "thread_id": "2026-11-04-news-israel-certifies-election-rejects-fraud-claims", "first_seen_date": "2026-11-04", "event_date": "2026-11-04", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["Israel", "Central Elections Committee", "Likud", "Benjamin Netanyahu", "Gadi Eisenkot"]}
{"id": "2026-11-04-news-greenland-parliament-approves-us-defence-deal", "date": "2026-11-04", "stream": "news", "headline": "Greenland parliament approves US defence deal", "summary": "Greenland's parliament adopted the trilateral defence agreement with Denmark and the US allowing two new US bases; Denmark votes on 19 November.", "url": "https://ina.gl/da/samlinger/oversigt-over-samlinger/2025/em2026/dagsorden/19/", "source_domain": "ina.gl", "tier": "T1", "tags": ["new source"], "topics": ["geopolitics"], "importance": 2, "display_body": "On Tuesday 3 November, Greenland's parliament, Inatsisartut, adopted the defence agreement between Greenland, Denmark and the United States, which allows two new US military bases on the island. The agreement, signed in September in New York by Trump, Danish Prime Minister Mette Frederiksen and Greenland's Prime Minister Jens-Frederik Nielsen, amends the 1951 US–Denmark defence treaty for Greenland. According to DW, it also covers Trump's \"Golden Dome\" missile defence system, keeps Greenland under Danish control and restates Greenlanders' right to self-determination. Pele Broberg, leader of the pro-independence Naleraq party, said Greenland had gained no economic benefit from it. It takes effect only after the Danish parliament ratifies it; a vote is scheduled in Copenhagen on Thursday 19 November.", "why": "the deal is the result of Trump's threats in January to take control of Greenland, and it gives the US a bigger military footprint in the Arctic without a change of sovereignty.", "thread_id": "2026-11-04-news-greenland-parliament-approves-us-defence-deal", "first_seen_date": "2026-11-04", "event_date": "2026-11-03", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["Greenland", "Denmark", "United States", "Inatsisartut"]}
{"id": "2026-11-04-news-spanish-transport-minister-quits-after-crash", "date": "2026-11-04", "stream": "news", "headline": "Spanish transport minister quits after crash", "summary": "Spanish transport minister Óscar Puente resigned on 3 November after the 28 October high-speed train collision that killed 69 people.", "url": "https://www.letemps.ch/articles/en-espagne-le-ministre-des-transports-presente-sa-demission-a-la-suite-de-la-catastrophe-ferroviaire", "source_domain": "letemps.ch", "tier": "T2", "tags": ["single-source"], "topics": ["politics", "world"], "importance": 1, "display_body": "Spain's transport minister Óscar Puente resigned on Tuesday 3 November, six days after a high-speed train collision on the Madrid–Seville line killed at least 69 people on Wednesday 28 October. He told parliament that a transport minister \"cannot continue in office with two accidents of this severity in less than a year\", referring also to the January derailment at Adamuz in Andalusia that killed 46. He also withdrew from the candidate lists for the early general election of Sunday 29 November. Early findings are that, after a computer failure stopped several trains, the control centre in Madrid wrongly told one train to continue, and it hit a stationary train. The government announced a €30 million aid package for victims and their families.", "why": "", "thread_id": "2026-11-04-news-spanish-transport-minister-quits-after-crash", "first_seen_date": "2026-11-04", "event_date": "2026-11-03", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["Spain", "Óscar Puente", "Pedro Sánchez", "Renfe"]}
{"id": "2026-11-04-news-federal-court-rules-out-most-website-blocks", "date": "2026-11-04", "stream": "news", "headline": "Federal Court rules out most website blocks", "summary": "Switzerland's Federal Supreme Court ruled that prosecutors lack a legal basis to order ISPs to block websites outside specific laws, upholding Init7's appeal against Vaud.", "url": "https://www.bger.ch/files/live/sites/tfl/files/pdf/de/7b_0052_2026_2026_11_04_T_d_14_15_40.pdf", "source_domain": "bger.ch", "tier": "T1", "tags": [], "topics": ["switzerland", "tech"], "importance": 2, "display_body": "Prosecutors cannot order internet providers to block websites except where a specific law allows it, the Federal Supreme Court said on Wednesday 4 November, upholding an appeal by the provider Init7 against the Vaud public prosecutor. In July 2025 the Vaud prosecutor ordered several providers to block access to the web addresses of an alleged online investment fraud (a DNS block, which stops the domain name from resolving). SRF reports that the fraud cost investors more than CHF 1.7 million. The prosecutor relied on the general rules on seizure in the Criminal Procedure Code. The court, in a judgment dated Monday 12 October (7B_52/2026), ruled that these rules are not a sufficient legal basis. Parliament has allowed blocking only in specific cases: unlicensed online gambling, illegal pornography and, temporarily, domains in unfair-competition proceedings. The court said blocking can be an effective tool against online fraud, but that deciding when to allow it, who orders it and on what conditions is for the federal legislator.", "why": "Swiss prosecutors lose a tool they had been using against online scams, and whether to give it back is now a question for parliament.", "thread_id": "2026-11-04-news-federal-court-rules-out-most-website-blocks", "first_seen_date": "2026-11-04", "event_date": "2026-11-04", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["Federal Supreme Court", "Init7", "Vaud"]}
{"id": "2026-11-04-news-pension-minimum-interest-rate-rises-to-2", "date": "2026-11-04", "stream": "news", "headline": "Pension minimum interest rate rises to 2%", "summary": "The Federal Council raised the BVG/LPP minimum interest rate from 1.25% to 2% from January 2027.", "url": "https://www.admin.ch/fr/newnsb/8DF8zxcfvu7l", "source_domain": "admin.ch", "tier": "T1", "tags": [], "topics": ["switzerland", "economy"], "importance": 2, "display_body": "The Federal Council on Wednesday 4 November raised the minimum interest rate on mandatory occupational-pension (BVG/LPP) savings from 1.25% to 2% from January 2027, above the 1.75% its advisory commission recommended on 4 September. The government says 2% matches the commission's own formula as of the end of September. It points to pension funds' average returns of 6.1% in 2025 and 5.4% to the end of August 2026, and an average funding ratio of 122.5% for funds without a state guarantee. In its words, the increase \"compensates the relatively high inflation observed since 2022\". The social partners had argued for rates from 1.25% to 3%. The minimum applies only to the mandatory part of retirement savings; funds set their own rate on savings above that level.", "why": "most employees in Switzerland will see a higher guaranteed return on their pension savings next year, and the rate is now above October's 1.5% inflation.", "thread_id": "2026-11-04-news-pension-minimum-interest-rate-rises-to-2", "first_seen_date": "2026-11-04", "event_date": "2026-11-04", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["Federal Council", "BVG"]}
{"id": "2026-11-04-news-bern-proposes-looser-co2-target-for-vans", "date": "2026-11-04", "stream": "news", "headline": "Bern proposes looser CO2 target for vans", "summary": "The Federal Council opened a consultation on loosening 2030 CO2 targets for vans and tightening them for heavy lorries, in line with the EU.", "url": "https://www.admin.ch/fr/newnsb/tDD-BZXX7QMM", "source_domain": "admin.ch", "tier": "T1", "tags": [], "topics": ["switzerland"], "importance": 1, "display_body": "The Federal Council on Wednesday 4 November proposed a weaker CO2 target for new vans from 2030 and a stricter one for heavy lorries, following the EU, and opened a consultation that runs until Thursday 18 February 2027. For light commercial vehicles registered from 2030, the target would be 108.7 g CO2/km instead of the 90.6 g/km in current law. For heavy lorries, the required cut in emissions from 2030 would rise from 30% to 45%. Importers of cars and vans would also be allowed to average their emissions over 2030–2032 instead of meeting the target every year. The government also wants to adopt the EU's standard energy label for vehicles.", "why": "", "thread_id": "2026-11-04-news-bern-proposes-looser-co2-target-for-vans", "first_seen_date": "2026-11-04", "event_date": "2026-11-04", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["Federal Council", "EU"]}
{"id": "2026-11-04-news-switzerland-taps-kerosene-reserves-again", "date": "2026-11-04", "stream": "news", "headline": "Switzerland taps kerosene reserves again", "summary": "The federal supply delegate authorised a second release of up to 50,000 m3 of kerosene from compulsory reserves due to refinery restrictions in France and Germany.", "url": "https://www.admin.ch/fr/newnsb/FBnf3YjJZk3R", "source_domain": "admin.ch", "tier": "T1", "tags": ["official PR"], "topics": ["switzerland", "economy"], "importance": 1, "display_body": "On Tuesday 3 November, the federal delegate for economic supply authorised up to 50,000 m³ of kerosene to be released from compulsory reserves until Sunday 29 November, the second release in a few weeks. This shortage is due to restrictions at refineries in southern France and Germany. Together with October's 30,000 m³, 17.4% of the kerosene reserve has now been made available; the delegate can authorise up to 20% before the economics department has to issue an ordinance. The release is mainly for Geneva and Zurich airports. Oil supply has been tight for months because the Rhine is hard to navigate, and reserves have been used four times since September.", "why": "", "thread_id": "2026-11-04-news-switzerland-taps-kerosene-reserves-again", "first_seen_date": "2026-11-04", "event_date": "2026-11-03", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["Geneva Airport", "Zurich Airport"]}
